TY  - CONF
TI  - TSAPI: Enhancing Existing OLTP Databases with Temporal Query Capabilities for Analytics
AU  - Ramanathan, C.
AU  - Kulkarni, A.
PY  - 2023
PD  - 
AB  - Time is an essential aspect of real world applications. A Temporal Query in SQL is composed of special database operators called temporal operators (e.g., AS-OF, WHEN, etc.). These are special operators that help applications perform time-based analytics on databases. Conventional OLTP (Online Transaction Processing) databases implemented using traditional RDBMS deal only with current data, that is, the data reflects modelled reality "as-of-now."On the other hand, OLAP (Online Analytical Processing) databases intended for analytics necessarily incorporate only historical data for analytics and do not cater to OLTP requirements that demands data values "as-of-now."Consequently, OLTP databases and OLAP databases are used for orthogonal purposes (transaction-processing vs historical analytics) and generally do not co-exist. In this paper, we demonstrate that the principles of temporal database design can be integrated seamlessly into existing OLTP relational databases to give the ability to run historical temporal query operators using (a) a Java-based static interactive tool and (b) a dynamic API called TSAPI (Temporal SQL API) for application development. Using this proposed approach, applications can access historical data using powerful temporal query operators without the need to implement expensive data warehousing infrastructure. The static interactive tool also automates and abstracts out the complexities associated with maintaining "in-place"historical data on entities and attributes in a selective manner exactly as per the requirements of the applications. Another unique feature of TSAPI is that it is backward compatible with existing relational database applications. In other words, the existing client applications and their underlying relational databases remain unaltered for regular use with standard SQL and JDBC. On the other hand, temporal aspects are injected into existing database schema and application code in the form of new query keywords that can be embedded in standard SQL for the applications. The conventional SQL language is supported as-is and the new temporal functionalities are offered using additional keywords for temporal data operations in a syntax just like the SQL language. The demonstration is done on a synthetic database implemented on MySQL.  © 2023 ACM.
